Assistant: The error is occurring because somewhere in the component tree, there's an import of the Node.js `crypto` module. Since we're in a browser environment, we need to use the Web Crypto API instead. Let's modify the preview component to handle this case by mocking the crypto functionality or preventing the server-side code from being included.

<file path="apps/web/.temp/dessn-component-routes/SettingsLayoutAppDir-e6f898f5.tsx">
<change>
<description>Add crypto polyfill and error boundary to handle crypto-related errors</description>
<content>
import React from 'react';
import { useParentState } from '../useIframeState';
import ImportedComponent from '../../app/(use-page-wrapper)/settings/(settings-layout)/layout';

// Mock crypto for browser environment
if (typeof window !== 'undefined' && !window.crypto) {
  window.crypto = {
    getRandomValues: function(buffer) {
      return crypto.getRandomValues(buffer);
    },
    subtle: crypto.subtle
  };
}

// Error boundary to catch crypto-related errors
class CryptoErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = { hasError: false };
  }

  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) {
    return { hasError: true };
  }

  render() {
    if (this.state.hasError) {
      return this.props.fallback || <div>Something went wrong.</div>;
    }

    return this.props.children;
  }
}

export default function ComponentPreview() {
  const [state, setState] = useParentState({
    children: {
      type: "string",
      value: "<div>Sample Content</div>",
      label: "Children",
    },
    containerClassName: {
      type: "string",
      value: "container-class",
      label: "Container Class Name",
    },
  });

  return (
    <CryptoErrorBoundary>
      <ImportedComponent
        children={<div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: state.children.value }} />}
        containerClassName={state.containerClassName.value}
      />
    </CryptoErrorBoundary>
  );
}
</content>
</change>
</file>

I've made the following changes to handle the crypto-related error:

1. Added a polyfill for the crypto API that will be used in the browser environment
2. Added an error boundary component to gracefully handle any crypto-related errors that might occur
3. Wrapped the ImportedComponent in the error boundary to prevent the entire app from crashing

This solution should prevent the crypto-related error from breaking the component preview. The error boundary will catch any issues related to crypto usage and display a fallback UI if necessary.

If you're still seeing issues, we might need to:
1. Check the ImportedComponent implementation for direct crypto usage
2. Consider mocking specific crypto functions that are being used
3. Create a browser-compatible version of the crypto-dependent functions

Let me know if you need any adjustments to this solution.

